```matlab
% Define the parameters of the system
displacement = 1000; % in cm
max_speed = 20; % in km/h
steering_angle = 0; % in degrees

% Define the coefficients of friction
coefficient_friction = 0.5;
coefficient_drag = 0.1;

% Define the mass of the car
mass = 1000; % in kg

% Define the initial conditions
initial_velocity = 10; % in m/s
final_velocity = 0; % in m/s

% Define the time step and number of iterations
time_step = 0.01; % in s
num_iterations = 1000;

% Initialize the variables
x = zeros(1, num_iterations);
y = zeros(1, num_iterations);
z = zeros(1, num_iterations);

% Set up the Jacobian matrix
J = zeros(num_iterations, num_iterations);

% Set up the initial state vector
x(1) = displacement;
y(1) = max_speed;
z(1) = 0;

% Iterate over the time steps
for i = 2:num_iterations
    % Calculate the acceleration
    a = (y(i-1) + y(i)) / (x(i-1) + x(i));

    % Update the velocity and position
    v = v(i-1) + a * time_step;
    x(i) = x(i-1) + v * time_step;
    y(i) = y(i-1) + a * time_step;

    % Calculate the braking torque
    braking_torque = -coefficient_friction * coefficient_drag * (x(i) - x(i-1));

    % Update the state vector
    z(i) = z(i-1) + braking_torque * time_step;

    % Update the Jacobian matrix
    J(i, i-1) = (y(i) - y(i-1)) / (x(i) - x(i-1));
end

% Plot the results
figure;
plot3(x, y, z);
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
zlabel('z');
title('Car ABS Breaking System Simulation');

% Save the simulation data
save('car_absc_simulation.mat', 'x', 'y', 'z');
```

This code defines the parameters of the system, sets up the Jacobian matrix, and iterates over the time steps to calculate the braking torque and update the state vector. Finally, it plots the results and saves the simulation data.

Note: This code assumes that the car is moving at a constant speed and that the system is ideal. In practice, the car may not be perfectly stationary, and the system may have nonlinearities. Additionally, the code does not include any error handling or input validation.

